# Digest 05: pattern 1, Is this normal, or is something wrong with my child?

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests/share-05.md. Yes posts: 198. Near posts: 62.

## Pattern 1, share 05: yes posts

### The caregiver worries that the baby is late to roll or struggles with tummy time.

10 of 198 posts.

These caregivers describe babies who stay on their backs, turn only onto a side, or cry rather than push up during tummy time. Ages run from two months to just over six months; some mention prematurity, body size, head shape or other babies rolling earlier. They describe floor time, assisted rolls and toys placed to encourage movement, and ask whether practice or professional help is needed.

- "I have tried everything. We practice every single day." [sr017-0004]

- "Just asking others because I keep seeing people talking about their 3 month olds rolling, and everyone stressed stop swaddling by 4 months due to rolling. Is it normal? We’re almost to month 5." [sr059-0131]

- "His doc is not concerned with either the not rolling thing and the head flat spot. But I find him really dismissive and I cannot get an indication to Physiotherapie." [sr041-0030]

- "My 10 week old hates tummy time on the floor. She lays there and just sucks on her arm. She isn’t overly interested in toys and loves looking at the bookshelf /playmat instead." [sr100-0032]

- "He also tries to turn onto his side, but he hasn’t rolled over yet. A few babies around his age in my neighborhood have already started rolling, so I’ll admit I’m feeling a bit anxious." [sr083-0054]

- "Tummy time at 2 month concern […] Should I panic???" [sr120-0036]

**But:** Some can perform the movement, but do it infrequently; the concern is consistency as well as absence. "He can do all of the 6 month milestones but it will be very infrequently and sometimes goes days in between doing them. […] For example rolling: he can roll both ways but he does it like once every couple of days and the rest of the time he just won’t do it." [sr130-0009]

Posts: 1sbv8fb 1sm5268 1ss3kix 1ttuvtb 1uih1pc 1vf9j2m 1vz2ymm 1wrxkgz 1x2ppx8 1x7j7tw

### The caregiver wants help when the baby rolls onto their tummy but cannot get back.

5 of 198 posts.

The baby can roll onto their tummy but becomes stuck, upset or sick, and the caregiver repeatedly turns them back. The babies are around four to five and a half months old, including one described as 16 weeks. Some ask how to teach the return movement; others fear suffocation or choking during sleep and struggle to stop watching.

- "LO is 5.5 months and has been rolling onto his front for about 3 weeks now. Trouble is he still cannot roll back no matter how much I try to help and encourage him. The result is constant rolling onto front, being happy for about 2 minutes (if that) then spitting up, fussing and eventually crying. I roll him back, 2 seconds later he's back on his front. This is my day ALL DAY and it's driving me slowly insane 😅" [sr061-0159]

- "I’m not sure what to do as she cannot roll back yet she gets a bit upset when she’s realises she is stuck. She also has reflux so even if I try all the tricks- paced bottle feeding, burp during and after feeds, upright for 30 mins etc. because she’s putting so much pressure on her tummy to roll she’s throwing up and sticking her face in it." [sr117-0024]

- "Sooooooo yea. Baby is rolling back to tummy but isn’t rolling tummy to back. He did roll tummy to back a few times a few weeks ago but hasn’t since. I’m having such awful anxiety that he’s going to get stuck like that and like suffocate or something (probably my PPA talking). Im terrified to leave him by himself for more than like 2 minutes at a time. Any support or advice?" [sr119-0028]

- "The doctor is not worried but I feel like I need to get a PT involved. Partially because this almost feels like a regression in his tummy time skills." [sr123-0004]

- "Should I just let her be? I keep trying to “practice” belly to back with her but she has such a yearning to be on her belly at all times lol but she does occasionally get very tired and angry to still be on her belly but then gets angry I rolled her back 🤦‍♀️" [sr024-0074]

**But:** One baby actively wants to stay on her tummy, so inability to roll back does not always mean she wants to be returned to her back. "Still can’t roll belly to back though and tbh shows zero interest in it even when being guided and encouraged to, she’s jumping straight to learning how to pivot on the belly." [sr024-0073]

Posts: 1t1ufvn 1uq5lny 1wo9ry6 1wqfin0 1wv5m3a

### The caregiver asks whether delayed sitting or an unusual way of getting around needs help.

8 of 198 posts.

Caregivers ask about babies who sit when placed but cannot get into sitting, do not crawl or pull up, or travel by rolling or army crawling. Most are around eight to eleven months, and one aunt describes a nephew approaching a year. They try floor time and moving toys out of reach, question whether weight or stronger language explains the difference, and seek encouragement alongside medical assessment.

- "My 10.5 month old isn't able to get into the sitting position from lying on her back. When put on her tummy she immediately rolls back to lying flat and only prefers to roll back on her right side. She's obviously not able to crawl or pull to stand. Though she's able to sit independently for longer period and can stand with support. She also slightly pulls herself to standing position when she's on my lap in front of sofa. When she's flat, she can come to sitting position by holding our finger." [sr002-0223]

- "My baby is really refusing to crawl! I've been putting toys slightly out of reach from him. He does that whole thing where he gets up on his hands and positions himself, but then he starts crying, plops down on the ground, rotates his body, and rolls. We've been doing this once or twice a day for the past week, and he just won't crawl! He'd rather plop and roll to the toys. If I move the toys when he rolls, he starts crying." [sr085-0123]

- "My 9 month old is army crawling, and he gets on his hands and knees and rocks back and forth. His 9 month WCC is soon and I’m not sure how to answer the question of “Is he crawling?”" [sr016-0027]

- "I am just wondering if this can really explain his motor delay. Did anyone have a similar situation? I think our pediatrician is way too chill and she gave us an appointment so late because she wanted to give him time to catch up, but honestly he doesn’t seem to be improving. I am thinking whether I should intervene." [sr058-0038]

- "Con casi 1 año el bebé está sentado y ya. No se desplaza ni se apoya. Yo he intentado alejar un poco sus juguetes de él pero por supuesto se frustra, llora... y el tummy time nada, no quiere hacer nada y llora." [sr041-0039]

**But:** An unusual crawl is not always accompanied by missing transitions or standing skills. "He gets from army crawling to sitting, sit to stand, cruises on furniture and occasionally does a few movements in 4 point crawl but then always ends up flopping down and commando crawling." [sr005-0194]

