### The caregiver wants the child to stand or walk without holding on.

6 of 198 posts.

Caregivers describe children who cruise, crawl quickly, climb or walk holding a finger but will not take independent steps or stand alone. Ages range from nine months to almost 17 months; one parent explicitly knows that not walking at 13 months is normal but still wants ways to encourage it. Practice, walkers, toys in both hands and separated chairs can be resisted, while comparisons at daycare or the park make the wait harder.

- "She will walk barely holding onto one of our fingers, but she refuses to go from sitting to standing independently or walking independently. […] We do so many different exercises (putting her between two chairs and slowly moving them apart - she just sits and pulls up on the other chair. Putting a toy in each hand, she just goes to her knees or stands there and doesn’t take steps, etc)." [sr134-0021]

- "We bought him a baby walker, we tried putting things on higher places for him to reach, we tried creating challenges, but this baby is a "no walking if I can prevent it" type of little fella. I really want to encourage him more but I have no idea what else I could do! Any tips?" [sr029-0045]

- "I feel so guilty because I've been dreading going. I get a lot of stares from people with his size and the fact he isn't walking independently yet. Inevitably, someone asks how old he is and seems very concerned or judgemental with his age. I then will end up feeling comfortable. I  cry on the way home frequently anymore." [sr043-0016]

- "My 14 month old isn’t standing independently and seems to prefer being on top toes when standing with support. Can cruise and does go on flat feet but often goes into tip toes. Can also walk flat while holding one finger. Getting concerned that there is a gross motor delay." [sr113-0203]

- "Obviously I’m super proud of her and okay if she waits a while. But I would LOVE to not have to carry her so much! Haha. 100th percentile baby here… my back, neck, and arms are screaming." [sr090-0103]

**But:** One parent later reports that the feared delay ended almost immediately after posting, followed by running. "Yes!! I was so stressed and tried everything to get her to walk. You won’t believe it, she took her first independent steps just 2 hours after I made that post! 😂 (Honestly, not exaggerating)" [sr003-0034]

Posts: 1sa92hx 1t6z8il 1tsfoi0 1vmpoki 1wno2ng 1xak9nn

### The caregiver worries that the child is not using their hands for expected tasks.

5 of 198 posts.

These posts concern purposeful reaching, holding a bottle, bringing objects to the mouth, stacking blocks or persisting with puzzles and sorters. The children range from three and a half months to almost 18 months, with different skills missing at different ages. Caregivers offer toys, model actions or ask whether an apparent skipped step counts as a delay.

