- "But for some reason, every time his grandpa (my father in law) holds him he cries hysterically until someone takes him away. After so many instances of him crying, I always make sure he's fully fed, changed, and recently taken a nap before handing him off to his grandpa and it doesn't matter. Within minutes he starts shaking and screaming." [sr098-0015]
- "Right now, we’re on the sofa watching Trash Truck with my boobs out, hoping he’ll eventually nurse. He’s gone to do it a few times, but then thought better of it and every now and then he’ll cry a little. It’s like he wants me, but he doesn’t believe it’s me." [sr116-0098]
- "It started around 4 months when we brought our son to a gathering with friends. If anyone would make eye contact with him and approach him he would pout his lip and start crying. Everyone tells us we need to let him be around people more often which really bums me out. Is this just a faze. Will it end?" [sr088-0087]

**But:** Closeness is not excessive in every case: one parent is troubled by a baby who tolerates being alone and dislikes long holds: "I know this sounds silly but I feel like my baby doesn’t care if my husband or I (or anyone) is there.  She is 12 weeks old and has been this way since she was a newborn. Perfectly ok being alone in her crib and she also gets mad if you hold her too long. Can tolerate it if you’re walking around with her but has her limits. […]" [sr037-0006]

