Parents ask about a possible first tooth at four or five months, whether a gum bump is a tooth or milk pearl, having no teeth at nine or eleven-and-a-half months, and grinding new teeth together at nine months. They compare visible gum changes, chewing and drooling with other babies' experiences. The grinding post also asks about tooth care and describes resistance to nappies and other routines.

- "My baby has been teething like crazy for almost a month (turned 4m Easter) it’s been brutal but no teeth! Well this I finally noticed swelling and it appears to be where her lower canines would be ?!

Has anyone else experienced this?" [sr009-0097]
- "my 5 month old baby has been drooling trying to chew everything and put everything in her mouth. So i felt around to see if i could feel anything and i did. I took a video and saw this but i think it’s a milk pearl i just want opinions." [sr038-0038]
- "My LO has been “teething” since 5 months old. She’s 9 months now and still no teefs ☹️ I’m not sweating it yet, but wondering when your babes got their first toof 🦷" [sr047-0038]
- "My baby got his bottom teeth a month ago now his top teeth are half way in. He’s been grinding them together, the sound of it URKS me. Is this normal??? Does it mean anything?? Also I’m a ftm, should I be brushing his new teeth every morning and night??? […]" [sr022-0071]
- "I can see two faint white spots where bottom teeth would be. The amount of drool is CRAZY. He’s also putting literally everything and anything in his mouth. I know that could just be exploring though?" [sr107-0003]

**But:** Late teeth do not always mean distress or worry to the parent: "My son is still without any teeth at at 11 months and half, not worried but curious if anyone else had a "late teether" (not sure if it is a word, English is not my native language , lol)." [sr101-0073]

