- "NOTHING will settle him. We’ve tried milk, food, Calpol, co-sleeping, cuddles, rocking. He just cries and cries basically until he passes out about 4/5am from exhaustion." [sr006-0063]
- "Is it developmental? Is there something wrong with him? This is harder than having a newborn. I am so exhausted and angry and sad." [sr006-0065]
- "I have read about night terrors a little but most people see them only once a week, where I am seeing this every single night.

Just looking for any tips, tricks, advice as this is really putting a toll on our family." [sr024-0266]
- "How can we sleep train this SUPER physically clingy toddler without traumatizing him?" [sr045-0019]
- "What am I missing? What should I definitely ask for? Doctors here are very dismissive, I want to get it right. We developed health problems from lack of sleep. My hair is falling out and I developed tinnitus and heart palpitations. We switch nights and basically the person who does the night, pulls an all nighter. I had to stop working because I'm not a functioning human being anymore and this gives financial stress as well. Because of this, we can't get daycare. We are desperate. We don't have family that can help, it's just us. […]" [sr095-0069]
- "Last night I sat in the recliner rocking her from 1–5 AM. If I even tried to stand up, she woke up screaming. My body cannot take sleeping or sitting with her in the recliner all night, and honestly it scares me because I know how unsafe it is to accidentally fall asleep there with her, especially with how much she wiggles and moves around." [sr116-0105]

**But:** For one child, holding immediately settles the crying even though a crib transfer restarts it: "Tonight she slept for maybe 3 hours and then woke up screaming bloody murder. The second I pick her up, she immediately calms down and passes TF back out in my arms." [sr116-0101]

