### They feel worn down by caring for their child and question whether they are doing enough.

4 of 62 posts.

These posts centre on the caregiver feeling defeated, depressed, isolated or overburdened, with developmental questions as part of that strain. They include two seven-month postpartum or baby accounts, an almost 18-month-old, and a family with a one-year-old and three-year-old. The speakers describe food preparation and treatment effort, medication attempts, unequal interaction or night care, work schedules and lack of support, including regret about becoming a parent.

- "Every time I feel like I am getting the hang of it, bam, something changes and I am out of my depth again. I just feel defeated by so much of it. And I’m only 7 months in! Does it pass?" [sr038-0037]
- "Except I don’t feel that way.  My mind is a complete war zone, and every day I have been a mother has felt like I made a giant mistake.  I’m constantly in high vigilance that he isn’t developing the way he should; I overlook every good thing he does and ask “why isn’t he crawling? Why isn’t he reaching up for me to pick him up? Why isn’t he imitating?”  I sob in the bathroom when I’m alone.  I sob in the car.  I’m afraid that the future I imagined won’t be the one that I’m faced with.  Why does it seem that every mom on the internet has this cosmic bond with their kid?  What kind of failure am I?" [sr065-0035]
- "[…] I'm also upset because our daughter isn't really talking yet, and the whole time I have her solo I'm talking with her, reading books, singing songs, etc. But when my wife gets home, she's scrolling on her phone and hardly talking unless I initiate it then wife will mimic me. I've talked with her about this and she apologizes and says she's just tired from work. Like WTF you don't think I am too and I can do all of this. We originally planned on having two kids, but some days I think I'm done after only one. I can't do this again." [sr070-0012]
- "The canoe tipped. We’re both now depressed, hating ourselves, and doing our best to fake it to the kids while they enjoy the moment in a way I can only envy now. I used to be able to do that… I’m getting crushed, drowned, just absolute despair." [sr110-0050]

