- "Thank you for sharing! Did he have a successful repair? I feel bad because I wonder if I somehow caused it 😣." [sr003-0130]
- "Anyone ever had a high percentile (90s) 2 year old that ended up leaning out and becoming a very average kid later? Is BMI now indicative of later?" [sr113-0067]

**But:** The urologist explicitly disputes the idea that the described foreskin is an abnormality or genetic issue: "[…] The urologist told us that he has a “normal” penis and that the hospital doctors don’t really know much about foreskin variations. He does not have hypospadias, chordee, or any genital abnormality. I asked if it’s possibly related to a genetic issue and the urologist told me it’s not and that it’s normal." [sr003-0128]

Posts: 1sedi5e 1wjbsti

### They worry about how their child is learning to hold up their body, roll, crawl or walk.

2 of 62 posts.

These parents ask how long it takes to move from early walking to a mature gait or from cruising and standing to walking. Both children's ages are unknown; the 14- and 20-month ages in the gait question are hypothetical examples. One parent reports a ChatGPT answer and asks for real experiences, while the other wants to know whether to get shoes for an outing; the reported ChatGPT answer is AI-derived information, not an observed child outcome.

- "From the time your baby starts walking 10+ steps to the time he starts walking without a baby waddle (like a duck) but walking relatively normally (like an adult), how many months did this take? […]" [sr056-0077]
- "ChatGPT says between 2-3 years old but starts fading around age 18 months old. How about some real data? I am asking for my baby." [sr056-0078]
- "How soon after standing independently/cruising did your baby start walking?" [sr127-0126]
- "Should I invest in shoes for him for Halloween? 👀 We aren't trick or treating but we'll be walking the neighborhood with some friends who also have babies." [sr127-0128]

**But:** The wait is not framed only as fear of delay; one parent also looks forward to carrying less: "Mine is in this phase. I'm looking forward to not having to carry him constantly. Ofc I love having him close! My arms are just tired." [sr127-0127]

Posts: 1ug2eu5 1x7ives

### They ask whether their child's way of playing or exploring is normal.

2 of 62 posts.

The main concern is an older child's independent imagination or drawing, rather than the younger sibling's infant milestones. One parent describes an almost two-and-a-half-year-old needing play initiated and modelled, with an 11-month-old sibling; another compares a four-year-old's frustration at drawing with a two-year-old's pictures. They describe creative toys, doing activities together, drawing demonstrations or tracing practice, and want to know what should emerge by itself.

- "My son, just shy of 2.5 yr old, seems to not have much of an imagination. He is the first child so maybe that is why? But we have lots of toys to support creativity like MagnaTiles, PlayDoh, Kinetic Sand, play food, lots of cars, and stuffed animals. He will only play with them if I initiate the activity and do all the work on “how” you could potentially play with them. I am happy to play with him and show him but it’s like he has no idea that he’s allowed to or that using imagination is even a thing. When did your child start using there imagination on their own? When can I expect this to blossom? […]" [sr077-0091]
- "My son’s preferred activities are just doing what I’m doing - chores, helping with food, exercising. He does enjoy playgrounds so that‘s a usual activity for us! Would love to see him make up stories or get wrapped up in a make believe world of his own." [sr077-0092]
- "I suggested drawing some different things like butterflies, flowers, hearts, and it's always "I don't know how!" But when I sit down and show her how, she won't follow the instructions, immediately gets frustrated and starts scribbling and crying." [sr115-0093]
- "My 2 year old has recently started drawing circles and adding eyes and mouths to them; she tells me she's drawing herself or her sister etc. so that's why I'm starting to wonder why my 4 year old is still so unwilling to just ... try stuff." [sr115-0095]

**But:** The older child in the drawing post does sometimes produce a picture they name: "[…] Occasionally she'll doodle something and tell me it's a rainbow or a whale or something... […]" [sr115-0091]

Posts: 1v6ljlr 1wkuffw

### They ask whether unusually strong reactions to sounds, sensations or stimulation will pass.

1 of 62 posts.

One parent describes a child who has just turned two and remains sensitive to people, mess and personal care. They report a clinician discussing possible sensory sensitivities, a low MCHAT score and reconsidering occupational therapy at two-and-a-half. The parent asks what helps tolerating uncomfortable experiences and compares this with their own childhood experience of being forced through panic.

- "He does think there may be a little bit of some sensory sensitivities (says it’s too early to say whether it’s sensory processing disorder and we’ll talk again at 2 1/2 we’ll check to see if he thinks she’d benefit from OT). He said a lot of kids do grow out of it if they’re regularly offered new/slightly uncomfortable sensory experiences." [sr054-0116]
- "We see her sensitivity manifest in shyness with new kids (she’ll cry if others get within 5 ft of her at the park but at daycare she plays and interacts with all her peers and some of the older kids), she doesn’t love her hands being dirty and is hesitant towards some messy play but not all, and she hates her hair being brushed, nails trimmed, etc." [sr054-0117]
- "If anyone has similar kiddos and what worked for them to get them used to sensory things that make them uncomfortable, whether they ended up with a diagnosis or grew out of it/learned to manage it I’d love to hear it! My parents basically just forced me to do things even if it caused panic attacks (I think it’s a reason I had so much anxiety as a kid even though their intentions were to help)." [sr054-0118]

**But:** The child's reactions differ between strangers and familiar peers, and the reported clinician view is not a current autism diagnosis: "[…] He doesn’t believe that she has autism at the moment because when she’s comfortable she’ll play with others, she talks well, she can do the actions to many kids songs, hits milestones etc. […]" [sr054-0115]

Posts: 1ubb5j1

### They cannot tell whether persistent crying or fussiness is a phase or something wrong.

1 of 62 posts.

One parent describes a high-needs four-to-six-month-old whose reflux, need for bouncing and sensitivity to outdoor conditions make attending a teenager's sports difficult. They say this has prompted fears of developmental delay and sadness about being less present for the older child. Passing the baby among relatives and the husband's help have not made the games easy.

- "[…] My baby is kind of colicky, he has silent reflux and he needs constant stimulation or lots of bouncing and walking around to keep him calm. […]" [sr021-0018]
- "[…] It’s got my husband and i down some rabbit holes wondering if he has a developmental delay. he isn’t really just sit on someone’s lap and watch the game type of baby right now, which has been hard, because i want to be present for both kids." [sr021-0018]
- "My son has another game this weekend and i feel bad knowing I probably just have to get a babysitter. What world do I live in where i have to get a babysitter to attend my sons’ game." [sr021-0019]

**But:** The parent reports help from the partner rather than saying they have none: "[…] My husband helps a lot so just clarifying that as that’s the first thing people ask. […]" [sr021-0018]

Posts: 1szg6xk

### They ask whether an unusual movement, posture or one-sided action is normal.

1 of 62 posts.

One parent asks when an initial right-sided approach to milestones should become symmetrical. The child's age is unknown. They report reading that starting on one side is common but also reading that babies should not have a side preference, and ask for research on when the sides should equalize.

- "My baby always start to do the milestones from the right part of his body. […]" [sr067-0001]
- "When should they equalize this? I have read around 7 months, but didn't find any research on this." [sr067-0002]
- "I have also read that babies should not have a preference on right and left, but all babies that I know had.... And most didn't have any significant torticollis." [sr067-0003]

**But:** The parent also reports having read an explanation that treats the initial asymmetry as common: "[…] And I have learn that this is pretty common that babies start with one side and then focus on the other." [sr067-0001]

Posts: 1uqokx2

### They ask whether their child's teeth are arriving or behaving normally.

1 of 62 posts.

One parent asks about timing and discomfort after a first tooth cuts through; the child's age is unknown. They describe several poor nights, a better night and renewed frantic chewing. They want to distinguish another tooth arriving from continuing discomfort as the first tooth grows out.

- "How long after cutting their first tooth did your babies cut their second tooth? […]" [sr076-0086]
- "[…] After a tooth emerges, does the discomfort continue for your baby until it has fully grown out?" [sr076-0086]
- "[…] We’re wondering if that means her second tooth is coming or if this is normal following a tooth first cutting through" [sr076-0087]

**But:** There was a long peaceful sleep between the disrupted nights: "[…] The night before last was peaceful and she slept for over 6 hours straight, […]" [sr076-0087]

Posts: 1v9794z

### They ask whether self-directed habits need stopping because they could hurt their child.

1 of 62 posts.

One parent describes a two-year-old pulling or twisting hair to self-soothe, with clumps of hair appearing over recent weeks and a changed hairline. They have contacted the pediatrician and ask what worked for other families. The post describes this as a recent habit rather than a longstanding sleep difficulty.

- "I’ve seen a few older posts about this problem with young toddlers pulling,twisting, rubbing their heads to help them soothe." [sr124-0092]
- "My kid does this and I’ve been finding little balls of hair for a few weeks in her room. I thought it was from the laundry. Now that my husband and I have put the pieces together, what worked for those looking for support in the past? The front of her hair looks like she got some bad bangs." [sr124-0093]
- "I’ve send her pediatrician a note as well for feedback." [sr124-0095]

**But:** The child is otherwise described as an independent sleeper: "She has always had great independent sleep skills, this just came up in the past month." [sr124-0094]

Posts: 1x16aoc

### They ask whether their child's speech is developing as it should.

1 of 62 posts.

One parent disputes speech-delay and possible ADHD concerns raised at a two-year checkup by an unfamiliar pediatrician. They describe clear phrases, newly emerging full sentences, book-based naming and a hyper, tired or overstimulated mood during the short appointment. The parent asks whether the assessment was justified and says relatives were surprised by it.

- "[…] She has started to used full sentences in the last month or so. A good example is if I say “ok let’s go eat” she will respond “yes mama let’s go eat, sit down” she points and says things she wants, when we are getting ready and are done getting her clothes on she will tell me “socks and shoes!” “Thank you mama” anytime I get her something." [sr132-0054]
- "[…] this woman ignores me completely, proceeds to tell me she thinks my child has a speech delay. Then brings up ADHD. Um… she’s TWOOO. I disagreed, respectfully and explained she was just in a toddler mood but she wasn’t always like that." [sr132-0057]
- "[…] She proceeds to tell me she should be saying atleast 200-300 different words by now, I mean I’m sure she’s up to 150 or so, but 300? Many of the “babbles” she says are her trying to say certain words but not pronouncing them properly which I thought also counted as words. She said no it doesn’t. Huh??? She said to consider speech therapy. […]" [sr132-0058]
- "I’m not trying to get in the way of my daughter’s progress. But i genuinely think this doctor was just wrong. Every person I’ve spoken to about this today was shocked, for example my cousin whose son was much far behind in speech at her age. Idk am I being crazy right now?" [sr132-0059]

**But:** The parent's account also acknowledges that some speech is still difficult to understand: "She still sort of sounds like she’s babbling sometimes but when you actually listen and pay attention, I started to understand what she was trying to say but still learning to pronounce. […]" [sr132-0054]

