### Other

1 of 62 posts.

One speaker gives general advice about bilingual language development rather than describing their own child's situation; the child's age is unknown. They dispute a belief that bilingualism itself delays speech, attribute positions to professional bodies and recommend evaluation across languages when there is a delay. These are the speaker's posted claims and advice, not independently checked research or a documented treatment outcome.

- "**The myth: bilingual kids develop language slower than monolingual kids.** […]" [sr119-0008]
- "A genuine speech or language delay in a bilingual child will show up in both languages, not just one. If a child is significantly behind across both languages they're exposed to, that's worth a real evaluation, ideally one that assesses all the languages the child speaks, […]" [sr119-0010]
- "If you're raising a bilingual kid and someone's told you to worry, or to drop a language, I'd push back gently on both. Happy to answer questions." [sr119-0012]

**But:** The advice does not dismiss all language concerns in bilingual children: "[…] If a child is significantly behind across both languages they're exposed to, that's worth a real evaluation, […]" [sr119-0010]

Posts: 1wpe6py

## Pattern 1, share 06: across the yes posts

| Tag | Posts |
|---|---|
| tried | 158 of 198 |
| others | 105 of 198 |
| bought | 3 of 198 |
| feeling | 131 of 198 |

Each yes post has one main form and a yes/no decision for each tag. Tried includes looking things up, seeking checks, practising skills and changing care; future intentions and passive observation alone do not count. Others includes reported advice, assessments, comments or actions by other adults, including help with care and online information; observing another child's abilities alone does not count. Feeling records the speaker's stated feelings, including curiosity, enjoyment or explicitly not feeling worried; the child's feelings alone do not count. Bought requires an explicit purchase or payment, including a purchase by family.

These are counts of posts in this assigned share of pattern 1 from the random sample of 20,000 posts, not counts of people or estimates of market prevalence. All 198 yes and 62 near posts were read in full as supplied; nothing in the share remains unread. For near posts, unknown ages stay unknown; ages mentioned only as hypothetical examples are not treated as the child's age.

### What they tried, in their words

- "We’ve tried all the pediatric OT tips like kneeling play, putting a toy on the other side of our leg, etc. He wants to hold hands and walk or stand while holding on to things. We recently borrowed a walker from his cousin and he flat out runs in the walker. Like he can seriously zoom around our kitchen to follow our dogs." [sr056-0095]
- "If we try to be interactive with him with mimicking and trying to get him to repeat sounds he looks at us like we are absolutely nuts and has no interest in repeating us." [sr049-0114]
- "I file down her nails as often as I can because she tends to scratch herself, mittens don’t stay on, onesies/long sleeves that fold over her hands she just pokes her fingers through and eventually her whole hand no matter how often you pull the cover back over." [sr066-0033]
- "I’ve been reading her every book about gentle hands and consent, singing songs and frequently reminders that we don’t touch. […]" [sr034-0020]
- "[…] After 3 days post vaccines I slowly started to practice with her rolling over a few times a day.. and by the 7th day she just did it her self. And now she’s fine, back to her normal self." [sr028-0015]
- "I currently have an early intervention appointment to get her evaluated. Did I over reach by referring her to early intervention?" [sr100-0037]
- "First we didn't link it to that toy, but now after having seen the same thing happening 6 times we decided to remove the toy and he stopped having these episodes. Then we gave it back to him as a test and he had another episode again." [sr113-0124]
- "So FTM to a 9 week old. Since day 1 he's been a kid who spits up. We tried regular enfamil in the hospital and he wasnt having it. They switched us to gentlease before we left and he's been on it ever since. As he's gotten older the spit up isnt every bottle anymore." [sr026-0032]
- "Tylenol and motrin no help at all. […]" [sr022-0016]
- "[…] And we even tried sleep training him multiple times over the past year and he’d stand at the edge of his bed crying and if he accidentally fell asleep, would wake back up and go back to screaming so we gave up." [sr098-0120]

### What other people told them or did

- "Pediatrician says not to worry until she turns 1, but everything online points to no babbling by 10 months being a pretty certain indicator of future delays, and if that's the case, shouldn't she be evaluated as soon as possible?" [sr012-0077]
- "Community health nurse measured it yesterday and said it’s 3.5cm x 3 cm and if it hasn’t reduced in size by 12 months, she will be getting scans and seeing a specialist. […]" [sr003-0053]
- "My 22 month old son when he gets extremely angry, he will flap his hands and stomp his feet. My sister immediately went “he probably has autism.”" [sr027-0131]
- "I’ve mentioned in to her doctor and he’ll reassure me that it’s normal but yet this doesn’t feel normal." [sr055-0007]
- "[…] Everyone tells us we need to let him be around people more often which really bums me out. […]" [sr088-0087]
- "[…] I called my pediatrician to get an OT referral but she said this is just typical toddler behavior and denied it. […]" [sr034-0021]
- "[…] He ended up “in the gray area” for most of it and the preschool said they’re not too concerned about it, but will reassess in a couple months. […]" [sr123-0083]
- "We recently had our first parent-teacher conference at daycare and were surprised to learn that our 20 month old daughter barely talks there, if at all." [sr048-0022]
- "FTM here of a 14 month old daughter and today my bf texted me to say that he thinks her "speech is behind". When I asked why he said because she doesn't say anything." [sr076-0102]
- "I was at a family thing and my sisters kid (11 months) was walking! I told her how great that was to have an early walker! She was confused and said this kid was the latest of all to walk (she has 2 other kids). Later my brother was asking me if I plan to start physical therapy soon!" [sr126-0027]

### What they bought or paid for

Only three yes posts explicitly describe purchases (including toys bought by family). The first three excerpts below support the bought count; the last two record borrowed gear and a researched price, and are not counted as purchases. Using a product, attending therapy or a class, and booking a visit are not treated as proof of payment.

- "[…] I bought a rail from Amazon that allows her to not fall off the bed […]" [sr006-0027]
- "Yeah that’s true. My family always buys him things that spin since he loves them so much lol I’ll tell them to start buying him other toys without spinning parts. He does like his Lovevery toys he gets" [sr030-0111]
- "[…] so i bought some teething toys which seem to be intriguing / new for them." [sr074-0058]
- "[…] We recently borrowed a walker from his cousin and he flat out runs in the walker. Like he can seriously zoom around our kitchen to follow our dogs." [sr056-0095]
- "Yes. This is something I’ve looked into. Part time pre-school in our area is $1500-$1700/month." [sr126-0048]

### How they say they feel

- "Please be kind because my mental health is failing over this. I’m losing sleep and I can’t enjoy my child because all I do all day is worry and analyze his every move. […]" [sr003-0184]
- "[…] I feel so anxious that I feel like throwing up, can't function. […]" [sr019-0006]
- "[…] I’m so burnt out, I feel so isolated." [sr055-0006]
- "I feel at such a loss and sick to my stomach on why this is happening and what to do. I’m at the point where I don’t want to take her to be around any other kids." [sr034-0022]
- "[…] I feel like a failure of a mother who's doing everything wrong, especially when I talk to other parents whose babies by this age were eating their own body weight in solids and taking themselves off to bed where they sleep through the night." [sr103-0005]
- "I’m also dealing with postpartum depression and I’m on medication for it, so I think this sudden change is hitting me extra hard." [sr099-0101]
- "Anyway, I’m now on Reddit seeking advice because I don’t know what else to do. I feel like no matter how hard I try it isn’t enough and dad is constantly upset about the lack of communication. How do I help my kids communicate?" [sr126-0046]
- "Thank you, he’s such a wonderful kiddo. I had pretty bad PPA and I feel like I missed out on his newborn times :( so I’m trying not to repeat myself" [sr030-0116]
- "[…] I feel very proud of my little girl and like she's just learning things at her own pace but today my partner asked ourselves whether we should be concerned and now I'm overthinking." [sr001-0092]
- "On one hand it’s freakin’ \\\*hilarious\\\* but on the other I lowkey feel judged by my kid for not having a clean enough house devoid of lint and random crumbs 🤣" [sr017-0141]
- "My son is still without any teeth at at 11 months and half, not worried but curious if anyone else had a "late teether" (not sure if it is a word, English is not my native language , lol)." [sr101-0073]


