# Digest 04: pattern 1, Is this normal, or is something wrong with my child?

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests/share-04.md. Yes posts: 199. Near posts: 62.

## Pattern 1, share 04: yes posts

### The parent asks whether the child’s babbling or words are late, meaningful enough, or in need of help.

22 of 199 posts.

These parents ask when sounds become babbling, when babbling becomes words, and whether inconsistent or imitated sounds count. The children range from about six months to nearly two years, including bilingual and trilingual homes; some parents want teaching ideas, others an evaluation or stories of later progress.

- "[…] I'm spiraling worrying she will never talk. […]" [sr117-0058]
- "She also does not use screens at all, and I stay at home with her and talk all the time. We do read 5-10 books a day and sing songs on repeat, I just feel like i’m missing something!" [sr018-0079]
- "[…] I'm wondering if it counts as a word, or a gesture. […]" [sr040-0019]
- "Basically title. We're also sorta trying to teach them a 2nd language and trying to mostly do OPOL but not perfectly. They were a preemie if that makes any difference. They do babble and make vowels and consonants sounds but nothing we can quite make out yet. Should we be concerned?" [sr108-0068]
- "However, I feel he does not say words with intention. For example, he can say mama papa but he does not look at mom/dad and call out to us." [sr031-0020]
- "My son just made 22 months and says over 50 words and understands everything. He says the words when he wants but then confidently speaks gibberish. We are teaching him/talking with him in 3 different languages. For those of you who had toddlers who spoke gibberish when did it turn into real speech?" [sr019-0105]

**But:** Some later updates describe improvement without claiming that the child has caught up completely. "[…] He’s def still behind the curve technically but I’m not nearly as concerned as I was when I made this post" [sr021-0029]

Posts: 1sfc5qg 1sksour 1suzjp8 1svtjx0 1szzzfo 1t28vx0 1tdi3wg 1tf51yt 1tl5x8r 1tu055m 1tvy7ue 1u2zx6l 1udy6xb 1upbn69 1usvkm5 1vsxvte 1wac03e 1wev2ul 1wnnhuo 1wpuiro 1wyqq9a 1x9zk3u

### The parent worries because speech has stopped, become less consistent, or changed.

8 of 199 posts.

The parent has heard sounds or words before and is unsettled when they disappear, come and go, or acquire repeated sounds. These posts span an almost-nine-month-old with pauses in babbling through toddlers around twenty months using fewer words or newly stuttering; one thirteen-month-old is waiting for an autism evaluation.

- "[…] My baby is now 13 months and maybe a week after his 12m appt he stopped saying his only two words. So it’s now been 3 weeks with no words whatsoever, just alot of babbling but no more “mama” or “dada”. […]" [sr090-0030]
- "[…] She will babble every day for a week straight and then go long stretches (sometimes a couple weeks) without babbling. […]" [sr107-0082]
- "So I have heard my 7/2024 baby say approx 70 words but the last couple of weeks he’s resorted back to pointing and grunting and whining and only uses a select few words (labeling things he knows, up, dada, sissy)." [sr002-0195]
- "[…] He’s been healthy and thriving in every other way and speaks more than 300 words easily and has been saying simple 3-4 word sentences for a while now…I just dunno why this stutter has come on legitimately overnight? […]" [sr035-0099]
- "[…] For example: “up” is “up-puh puh puh”. “milk” is “milk-kuh kuh kuh kuh”. “Walk” is “walk-kuh kuh kuh kuh”." [sr053-0100]

**But:** One parent later reports babbling and a possible first word after the earlier silence. "Hi, mine is just turning 1 year and babbles like an absolute lunatic lol. She randomly started one day when she was just shy of 11 months. She's just started saying what we're pretty sure is her first word, "bye bye", so I guess she just wasn't interested in language for ages, then learned it all in one go." [sr011-0147]

Posts: 1se03gh 1sp1civ 1thhyql 1u9k6jx 1uozc1u 1vixa39 1wh0uad 1x8cfgo

### The parent asks whether missing gestures, name response or back-and-forth interaction signal a problem.

19 of 199 posts.

The main uncertainty is whether the child shares attention and responds to people: pointing, waving, clapping, imitation, eye contact, name response and conversational turn-taking. Ages run from a three-week-old whose parents worry about recognition after surrogacy to a twenty-month-old seeking help with communication; some describe several reassuring social or motor skills alongside the missing ones.

- "One thing that worries us is that he does not follow pointing at all. At best, he sometimes looks at the finger itself, but not toward the referenced object. He also does not seem to do most socio-communicative gestures/behaviors for his age: no waving, no pointing himself, no showing us objects, no nodding/shaking head, no socially motivated clapping, and he also does not follow another person’s gaze." [sr030-0122]
- "My 17mo almost never looks at me when I call him by his name. The times he does, I wonder if it was just a coincidence anyway." [sr123-0098]
- "But I can’t make noises at her and then she makes noises back." [sr099-0110]
- "I feel like I'm not doing enough or that he just doesn't like us or struggles to think of us as his parents. (As much as a newborn can think)" [sr050-0071]
- "Thank you for the great tips regarding pointing. I shall try that.. I’m a first time mom here.. I really didn’t know that pointing was such an important skill to learn for kids. I was concerned at 15 months when he was not responding consistently to his name. When I asked the pediatrician she said for early evaluation I need to wait unil 18 months. I wish I had started earlier.." [sr008-0018]
- "However, I want to help my child in anyway. So I booked an evaluation 2 weeks from now, I was surprised I didn’t need a referral but I do have to pay out of pocket. Am I wrong for this? I know he could start to progress soon, but I am concerned." [sr112-0051]

**But:** A later update in one post describes the previously missing gestures and words appearing. "Great! Almost 18 months old, has about 60 words, pointing, waving and everything." [sr017-0043]

Posts: 1sc58wd 1sfgaap 1sgwgpt 1sr5fep 1st2351 1t9hzda 1trcsoy 1u0u3ps 1u1lpzp 1u5eh8n 1u8qcap 1ua5hy0 1ukw9gy 1vzpt04 1vzwon3 1wbccmn 1wk8cd3 1x05g45 1xck9ko

### The parent worries about the child’s development across several areas.

5 of 199 posts.

These parents put several observations together and ask whether there is a wider developmental problem, rather than asking only about one milestone. The posts include screening results at twelve months, a quiet five-and-a-half-month-old with several normal test results, a nine-month-old with body and weight-bearing concerns, and a seventeen-month-old not yet walking or speaking; social media also prompts a broad comparison spiral.

- "[…] I'm scared. I've been lacking in some areas due to PP anxiety and depression but now I feel like I completely fucked up my child's development. […]" [sr052-0064]
- "Like... Could these things indicate a bigger problem? Or am I just overreacting?" [sr088-0135]
- "I am trying my best, really. My baby is 17 months and still not walking on her own (she will push a stand up walker and walk around the edge of furniture a little bit- she also walks on her knees a lot) or saying any words or animal sounds. She uses gestures sparingly. She still wakes up multiple times every night, she barely eats solid foods some days and even when she does eat I wonder if she’s getting enough." [sr092-0005]
- "[…] Chromosomal microarray and Fragile X testing came back negative. […]" [sr126-0082]
- "Then I get on social media and babies are talking full sentences, or walking, drinking from a cup easily, using lots of gestures like clapping and pointing, and just doing all of these things that my son can’t do yet. And it makes me feel like such a failure as a parent or that I could have done more. And then on top of that it’s like every other thing is a potential sign of autism and that adds another level of worry." [sr037-0027]

**But:** The parent whose worries begin online explicitly says they are otherwise unworried about the child’s health. "Everything is fine health wise with him and he’s such a happy baby I don’t have any worries until I get online and then the what ifs and why didn’t I’s come full force." [sr037-0028]

Posts: 1tnt2nf 1u7sn8u 1vnhgzp 1vp90by 1x5l3hw

### The parent worries that a movement or practical skill is late and asks how to help.

15 of 199 posts.

The child is not yet rolling, moving into sitting, crawling, bearing weight, pulling up or walking independently, and the parent wants a timeline or a way to help. Children range from five months to almost eighteen months; some can do a skill when placed or supported but do not initiate it, and others are compared with siblings or younger relatives.

- "Baby turned one year last week. She sits without assistance, but only if I help her get into that position. She stands for short periods of time holding against a table, but again, only if I put her there. She only rolls once every other day. She can booty scoot across a room in record speed, but she doesn’t crawl in the traditional sense. She can’t sit up from a lying down position, and she freaks out when she is put on her back." [sr016-0133]
- "My 6 month old hasnt really started to roll. She can roll to her sides and likes to sleep on her sides. If I give her a push when shes on her side she will go back to tummy. She has not figured out at all how to do tummy to back. I feel likes shes behind and Im not doing enough to help her learn. Any tips?" [sr040-0020]
- "My 9 month old recently had her check up at the doctor, and when I was asked if she was trying to pull to stand, I told her no because she’s not, and the doctor got on to me and gave me a sheet of exercises and told me she needed to be walking at her 12 month check up." [sr028-0123]
- "My LO took her first steps around 14 months and felt like the first two weeks she was making great strides, but now over 3 months later she’s still not confident walking independently. She relies heavily on holding our hands and if we encourage her to take a few steps on her own, she just resorts back to crawling. Would love some insight! Doctors aren't concerned, and we would love to help her build that confidence!" [sr061-0109]
- "I obviously know that daycares have been making it work with lots of kids for decades but I just feel like she is going to get trampled and have her hands stepped on or something. she has taken a few steps but just isn’t super steady yet. I know I shouldn’t be considering keeping her out for another month - maybe seeing the other kids will help her get speedier?" [sr109-0131]

**But:** Some parents explicitly say they are not particularly worried and are asking for other families’ timelines. "[…] I know we’re still well within the acceptable age range for rolling, and I’m not particularly worried." [sr039-0099]

Posts: 1sfqwkg 1sxonb9 1tb4mm0 1tljpdx 1tpon4a 1twhq15 1u6kyv3 1umz7h6 1v8wsz6 1vbpt45 1vi3ak0 1w4wbdm 1w6txbz 1wfq81i 1wo6mor

### The parent asks whether an unusual movement pattern, skipped step or unequal use of the body is normal.

6 of 199 posts.

The worry concerns how the child moves: standing before crawling, refusing to roll despite crawling, toe walking, pausing crawling attempts, or favouring one arm or leg. These children are mostly around seven or eight months, with one twenty-three-month-old toe walker; the feared explanations include a missed hip problem or cerebral palsy.

- "We were referred to neuro, with the appointment tomorrow, but after reading a lot this last couple of months I’m sure she’s going to be diagnosed with CP, and I’m devastated." [sr128-0032]
- "Now she doesn’t do that anymore. She still army crawls. When she crawls this way she pushes only with one leg and drags herself forward with her arms.  
I also noticed she tries to get knees under at times to go on all fours but has trouble getting her right knee under." [sr069-0113]
- "my nearly 8 month old only flaps or splashes or bangs/bats his left arm. he does it a lot when he is happy but only the left arm. he seems to be able to use both arms pretty equally, reaching with both and eating with both (maybe slight more with the right hand)" [sr096-0027]
- "is this normal for a baby to refuse to roll? I’ve been trying to get him to but he refuses to. he knows how to sit on his own,crawl, and even lift himself up. whenever he does wants to start crawling he does a literal sit up then gets into position he refuses to roll" [sr085-0126]
- "[…] My 23 month old used to to walk 100% of the time and he has now managed to get it down to about 30% of the time. […]" [sr085-0002]

**But:** Some describe other movements and strength as intact despite the asymmetry that worries them. "both seem equally strong on tummy time and he can push up with them." [sr096-0028]

Posts: 1trshuu 1uv70xu 1vb00fs 1vhfxoi 1vy8as2 1x4dfyw

### The parent asks whether the child’s head control during tummy time is developing normally.

5 of 199 posts.

The parent asks how much head lifting or control should be present and whether support, extra practice or physiotherapy is needed. Babies range from seven weeks to five months; some lift their head when carried or supported but not flat on the floor, while one parent reports poor head control when holding the baby too.

- "It’s not as if he hates it either when he’s on my chest/tummy time. He just doesn’t have the strength to turn his head. Or sometimes he just squishes his head face down." [sr109-0075]
