## Pattern 1, share 04: near posts

### The parent asks whether frequent waking or broken sleep is a regression and how to get longer stretches again.

20 of 62 posts.

Parents describe good stretches being replaced by frequent wakes, failed transfers or short naps, or ask what another regression could do to an already poor sleeper. Ages run from nine weeks to a two-year-old, including several four-, six- and eight-month questions; attempted solutions include routines, schedule changes, feeding, pacifiers, rocking, contact naps and bedsharing.

- "I feel like I’m constantly trying to troubleshoot wake windows, 3 vs 4 naps, overtired vs undertired, bedtime timing, sleep pressure, Huckleberry sweet spots… it’s exhausting." [sr055-0121]
- "I said it’s a 4-month thing but it feels like there’s always some kind of “regression” happening: 4-week, 6-week, 11-week, you name it." [sr033-0088]
- "\*\*Am I creating "bad habits" by feeding her to sleep right now?\*\* I'm terrified of creating a permanent sleep association, even though she doesn't do this during the day." [sr045-0063]
- "Should I be expecting him to wake up more than he already does? Is there anyone else in the same boat or had a similar experience? What actually happens to babies during regression periods who don’t have long stretches of sleep? 😂😂" [sr078-0023]
- "This is fucking up our relationship. Really taking its toll on us both." [sr079-0039]
- "Yeah I've been messing around with nap timings and duration for a while now but nothing seems to make any difference. Sometimes he'll have 3 hours of nap then sleep 10 hours overnight, sometimes he'll have only 1.5hrs nap and only sleep 8hrs overnight, and vice versa, it just seems so random. […]" [sr132-0028]

**But:** The worsening can be intermittent rather than every night. "[…] But then the next night she'll have a longer 6 hour stretch followed by a 4 hour stretch. I'd say it's about 50/50 if she'll sleep through those longer stretches or act like a newborn again." [sr049-0138]

