### The parent asks how to manage sleep when rolling or moving arms keeps the baby awake.

4 of 62 posts.

Movement is the immediate obstacle to sleep: flailing or a startle wakes the child, an arm knocks out a pacifier, or a newly rolling baby remains stuck on the tummy. Ages range from three weeks to five-and-a-half months; parents ask about freeing the arms, transitional swaddles, repeating a sleep-bag attempt or repeatedly flipping the baby back.

- "For bed time though we swaddle and she sleeps great for most of the night. She sleeps in a pack n play (hated the bassinet) and today we were in there doing tummy time and she rolled from tummy to back, so this means it’s time to stop swaddling. A lot of times by morning her arm will break free from the swaddle and she will be grunting and waving it around and then wake herself up." [sr008-0042]
