# Digest 05: pattern 1, Is this normal, or is something wrong with my child?

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests/share-05.md. Yes posts: 198. Near posts: 62.

## Pattern 1, share 05: yes posts

### The caregiver worries that the baby is late to roll or struggles with tummy time.

10 of 198 posts.

These caregivers describe babies who stay on their backs, turn only onto a side, or cry rather than push up during tummy time. Ages run from two months to just over six months; some mention prematurity, body size, head shape or other babies rolling earlier. They describe floor time, assisted rolls and toys placed to encourage movement, and ask whether practice or professional help is needed.

- "I have tried everything. We practice every single day." [sr017-0004]

- "Just asking others because I keep seeing people talking about their 3 month olds rolling, and everyone stressed stop swaddling by 4 months due to rolling. Is it normal? We’re almost to month 5." [sr059-0131]

- "His doc is not concerned with either the not rolling thing and the head flat spot. But I find him really dismissive and I cannot get an indication to Physiotherapie." [sr041-0030]

- "My 10 week old hates tummy time on the floor. She lays there and just sucks on her arm. She isn’t overly interested in toys and loves looking at the bookshelf /playmat instead." [sr100-0032]

- "He also tries to turn onto his side, but he hasn’t rolled over yet. A few babies around his age in my neighborhood have already started rolling, so I’ll admit I’m feeling a bit anxious." [sr083-0054]

- "Tummy time at 2 month concern […] Should I panic???" [sr120-0036]

**But:** Some can perform the movement, but do it infrequently; the concern is consistency as well as absence. "He can do all of the 6 month milestones but it will be very infrequently and sometimes goes days in between doing them. […] For example rolling: he can roll both ways but he does it like once every couple of days and the rest of the time he just won’t do it." [sr130-0009]

