### The caregiver wants to know why the baby is not cooing or babbling more.

7 of 198 posts.

Caregivers describe babies from 13 weeks to nine months who grunt, squeal, growl, make vowels or use one repeated sound rather than the cooing or varied consonant babble they expected. Several already talk, read, sing, narrate or practise sounds and ask what else could help. They want other parents’ timelines and to know whether to raise the issue at the next appointment.

- "My baby is 13 weeks and she still doesn’t coo. She has a floppy airway but I’m not sure if that make it harder to coo. She grunts sometimes and I’ve heard little noises a few times. She does look at us and smile but I’m just sad about the cooing. Anyone else’s baby no cooing and should I be concerned?" [sr022-0044]

- "I am reading to him a lot, talking to him face to face, playing with him, singing nursery rhymes... nothing seems to be working." [sr034-0048]

- "My baby is 6.5 months old, and he’s so far hit all his milestones. I was reading that they should be babbling (bababa, dadada for example) but I noticed my son doesn’t totally do this. He is very vocal and does the pterodactyl noises a lot and yells and likes to hear his voice, but doesn’t “babble” like with consonants yet. It’s a lot  of “ahhh” still. Is that concerning?" [sr055-0053]

- "We've been trying to encourage more babbling by talking to her constantly, reading books, singing songs, narrating what we're doing, and repeating sounds like "ma" "ba" and "pa" but she still seems to stick with just "dada" and unable to say anything else." [sr072-0025]

- "I’m growing concerned to to the point where it’s starting to stress me out because I see other babies her age that aren’t babbling but at least making vowels sounds and she doesn’t have a visit with her ped for another 2 weeks. Does anyone have any experience with this?" [sr116-0130]

**But:** One worried parent already reports several syllables and physical progress while asking when babbling will begin. "I know this is a 9mo milestone, but I’m so nervous 🥲 she’s crawling, pulling to stand, etc. she laughs, pterodactyl screams, says “mama” “baba” “bubu” and recently started practicing the “da” sound." [sr068-0013]

