### The caregiver wants more spoken words or wants the child to use words to communicate.

11 of 198 posts.

Caregivers ask whether approximations count as words, why mama or dada is missing, and why a child with vocabulary does not request things or answer conversationally. Ages range from almost eleven months to 23 months. Understanding, signs or memorised labels may be stronger than spoken requests or combinations, and parents describe reading, narration, prompting and considering early intervention.

- "My 17 month old only reliably says a few words - mama, cheese, pop pop, book, and bread. However he also makes the first letter sound for a lot of words. For example, he’ll make the “f” sound to refer to fish, friends, feet, or he’ll make the “s” sound to refer to the sun, his socks and shoes, sticks, our cat Sampson (he makes a double s sound for him 😂), “ch” for charlie( our other cat), “t” for tree, tractor, etc. Is this normal and do these count as words?" [sr008-0036]

- "However, my concern is that her expressive speech is mostly all prompted (although she will occasionally spontaneously label some things on her own). For example, majority of the time, i can only get her to say “more” or “open” if i tell her to say it or model it. Or if i say “can you say…” she will say it (if she’s in the mood to play that game lol) or “it’s a….” And let her fill in the pause." [sr044-0031]

- "My 15 month old loves to talk. He can count to 10, recognize numbers, says over 20 animal names and the sounds they make, says colors and shapes, names foods...but absolutely refuses to say basic words like "yes", "bottle", "up", "dada", "more" etc unless he's singing a song with those words in it." [sr109-0133]

- "I feel horrible like I have failed her in some way. I also feel that she gets so frustrated by not being able to communicate and it makes me sad." [sr084-0077]

- "Am I expecting a Shakespearean communicator and the babbling counts? or is this something I should maybe bring up to doctor" [sr086-0108]

- "Now I feel preet sad, embarrassed and discouraged." [sr015-0016]

**But:** The concern is not always an absence of language: one child has many words, some combinations and learned phrases. "My LO will be two in a month, she is so social and chatty says a lot of words, too many to count, but she basically says gibberish and then a word, gibberish, word. She combines a little bit, like car beep beep, cow moo moo. She says “ready set go” and ‘counts’ to 3. Knows some colors." [sr027-0179]

