### The caregiver worries because sounds or words the child used before have become less frequent.

4 of 198 posts.

These caregivers notice babbling stop, be replaced by squeals, or become less distinct after previously hearing recognisable words. The babies are six to almost eight months, and the toddlers are 15 and 17 months. They wonder whether attention has shifted to other skills, whether this is regression, or whether mixed opinions about autism apply.

- "My baby will be 8m in a few days. Babbling has dropped to zero for the past month. I'm wondering if it's because she's focusing on mastering other skills\*." [sr050-0239]

- "He used to babble too which has stopped and it seems to be replaced by either an ehh shout or a squeal thats so loud it physically hurts my ears." [sr065-0011]

- "Recently in the last week or so, he’s reverted back to using mostly grunts and babbles for any expressive language. He will say yeah or nah appropriately. He completely understands what we’re saying, follows directions, and is still using gestures. He also is still extremely talkative, just less distinction in the sounds." [sr034-0078]

- "but she just doesn’t talk much. she does a lot of babbling, humming, and what my friend referred to as “vocal stimming” but i’ve always seen that kind of as a form of her babbling. what concerns me is she has learned words and just stopped using them." [sr130-0031]

**But:** One parent says the words have not disappeared completely and new words are arriving at the same time. "now, it’s not so much. she won’t say it when prompted, but it’s not a total loss as she’ll say it every now and again along with dada. but at the same time she’s learning other words. she says no, and shakes her head properly with it. she also says yes. she says “woah” appropriately , “hey” appropriately. she also has tried other words recently like “turtle” and “duck” also appropriately like when playing with her animal toys." [sr130-0032]

