### The caregiver asks whether the child looks, listens, smiles or connects with people enough.

22 of 198 posts.

Caregivers ask how much eye contact, response to a name, smiling, laughing, affection or back-and-forth interaction to expect, from newborns to an 18-month-old and a later update at 14 months. Some describe inconsistent responses or reduced sociability; others have a quiet, content baby and wonder whether that itself is unusual. The group also includes an alternating newborn hearing-screen result, questions about corrected age, and a parent attributing changed eye contact to the timing of vaccination.

- "Hi! My LO will be 10mo next week. It seems like she has a very inconsistent response to her name. If we are both in her play pen and she is busy playing and I say her name she almost always ignores me. However if she’s in her high chair without distraction and I say her name she looks immediately." [sr122-0073]

- "There was a period he was excited to see us, but now instead of being excited he will hold up say a car in his hand and yell “car car car”.  While I’m happy he’s acknowledging us, I can’t help but worry that this isn’t normal." [sr022-0153]

- "However he seems to go through sociable and then very unsociable periods, and I cannot see a pattern, it's not linked to tiredness or hunger or teething or bordem (well maybe bordem, he gets bored easily!)." [sr098-0041]

- "FTM here and I’ve been worried about my 7 months daughter for the last month or so. She used to be super social and smile at everyone and for the past month or so even I have a hard time getting her to smile or laugh." [sr107-0014]

- "I hate to give into fear but is this the result of the vaccines? It’s just weird to me that it’s directly after and he wasn’t doing this before. Please tell me I’m imagining things and he’s ok?? I wanted to come here before I speak to my husband because he’s panickier than I am." [sr039-0014]

- "Iron levels were normal. […] Chromosomal microarray and Fragile X testing came back negative." [sr126-0077]

- "The nurses said it was unusual and referred us to an audiologist." [sr091-0143]

**But:** One parent reports a substantial increase in connection after an initially serious infancy, without claiming to know the eventual explanation. "The last two months has been quite wild in that he is now seemingly understanding a lot of things we’re saying, is conversationally babbling and say some words and gestures etc, and starting to interact with us like he’s his own person. It genuinely feels like his brain has kicked up an extra gear." [sr131-0064]

