Caregivers ask about flat spots, a preferred head position, a groove, a cone-shaped newborn head, unequal-looking eyes during a smile, a downturned mouth or a tongue that stays out. Ages range from 12 days to 13 months, with the head-groove post giving only the broad baby stage in its header. Some seek positioning advice or helmet experiences; one fears an untreated tongue-tie will affect later speech and recalls being teased.

- "My son is 4 months old and we’re concerned about a mild flat spot. He has always favored sleeping with his head to one side. I talked to his ped and had an apt with prosthetics. We’ve been trying to correct the problem the last 5 weeks, but I don’t think it’s changed anything. He will get fitted for a helmet in a couple weeks if they determine the shape is still a concern." [sr015-0026]

