### The caregiver cannot explain or soothe the baby’s crying and asks whether it is normal.

18 of 198 posts.

Caregivers describe crying that feels excessive, unexplained or hard to stop, sometimes after a previously easier period. Ages run from five days to 13 months, with newborn witching hours, later whining and crying around new skills all represented. They try feeding, gas or reflux approaches, rocking, contact naps, outings and other soothing; one fears harm after vaccination, while others ask whether crying predicts a difficult temperament.

- "What troubles me the most is that I can’t figure out what’s wrong. I read that babies get extra fussy between weeks 6-8, but I’m trying to understand why to make sure I can do something to help her." [sr039-0085]

- "I’m running out of ideas to soothe him as nothing seems to work for more than 20 minutes. I’ll feed him, wind him, change his nappy and finally manage to settle him to sleep only for him to wake up 20 minutes later regardless of whether I dared to put him down and the whole cycle starts again. Toys? Cool for a few minutes then crying again. Same for music, same for bouncing, same for walks in the pram. I think we’ve covered literally everything including taking him to the GP and she couldn’t find anything wrong with him. Any advice would be greatly appreciated, Mummy is at her wits end! 😫" [sr073-0028]

- "I’ll try multiple methods of soothing him — holding, wrap, pacifier, swing, rocking, music, singing, nap, bath, swaddle, white noise — but sometimes I strike out completely and just accept that I’ll be listening to him scream for awhile until he wears himself out and eventually accepts a nap or a bottle." [sr065-0025]

- "I’ve spent way too much time reading scary things online about vaccines and how they supposedly affect babies physically and mentally, and now I’m sitting here wondering if I somehow made a terrible decision and hurt my own child. I’m terrified that this is going to become his new personality and that he’s going to be miserable and screaming all the time." [sr096-0050]

- "I am afraid that he will be moody/whiny into his toddlerhood. Anyone who has similar experience how did the toddlerhood went?" [sr113-0074]

