### They ask whether their child's speech is developing as it should.

27 of 198 posts.

These posts ask about missing consonant babbling, first meaningful words, small vocabularies, two-word combinations and speech that differs by setting. Ages run from six months to 22 months, with one parent also describing an almost four-year-old sibling. Some parents count gestures or sounds, read and model words, or arrange hearing tests and speech evaluations; others want to know whether bilingualism, ear infections or concentrating on movement explain the timing.

- "My almost 12 month old is SO quiet. He has maybe babbled a handful of times over the past few months and it’s always been either gagaga or bababa. Never mamama or dadada. […]" [sr079-0001]
- "What actually counts as "talking " and are all the parents telling me their kids said 5-10 words at this stage delusional or is the bar for what counts for a word at this point just something I am not understanding? I'm started to get worried she's missing this milestone." [sr041-0012]
- "I’m reading mixed things online if “hi, mommy/daddy” and “thank you, mommy” are true two word combinations. I’m feeling discouraged because I thought we were on track." [sr010-0002]
- "We are talking to him and reading books every single day and even though we keep on repeating the same words for days (like door or cat) he still doesn’t copy us." [sr125-0081]
- "We recently had our first parent-teacher conference at daycare and were surprised to learn that our 20 month old daughter barely talks there, if at all." [sr048-0022]
- "We have done an autism screening (no concerns) and have started speech therapy after a VERY long wait list, just for them to tell us he is hitting all of the milestones for his age and they don’t think he needs speech therapy. […]" [sr126-0043]

**But:** The requests do not all describe continuing delay or personal alarm. One follow-up reports progress, and another parent explicitly says they are not worried: "Lol, yeah she's a 15 month old now who never shuts up. Babbles constantly and has about 5 words. […]" [sr012-0079] "[…] I want to add that I’m not worried or concerned. I know kids do things on their own times, especially boys. […]" [sr061-0056]

