### They ask whether their child's size, head shape or physical features signal a problem.

13 of 198 posts.

These posts focus on height, head size or shape, a soft spot, genital size, or hand creases that a parent fears could indicate a condition. Ages range from eight weeks to 23 months. Parents compare percentiles, growth curves, family heights and physical features, sometimes after a clinician raises a concern and sometimes despite reassurance; the posts also include deciding against a helmet and considering ways to make a child taller.

- "I’m obviously freaking out and hate the feeling of uncertainty that will stay in my mind until we get a scan. I’m also suspicious of how subjective the whole examination seems […]" [sr069-0069]
- "Community health nurse measured it yesterday and said it’s 3.5cm x 3 cm and if it hasn’t reduced in size by 12 months, she will be getting scans and seeing a specialist. […]" [sr003-0053]
- "Nevertheless, I'm worried. There's **a lot** of discrimination against short males in society. I want to help my baby as much as I can so he can be happy." [sr002-0256]
- "After several examinations of my sons head, i decided to not use a helmet to fix the flat spot on my sons head." [sr006-0228]
- "He is not cut and he is ten months old. It has always touched his belly button. It’s about the width of a paper large Starbucks straw. It’s maybe three inches?" [sr010-0220]
- "[…] But then tonight I noticed he has a  simian crease on both hands. He’s healthy weight, seems to have good muscle tone." [sr055-0001]
- "[…] Am i doing something wrong with his diet?   
Any suggestions that helped you, im thinking about maybe running some tests i dont know." [sr128-0008]

**But:** An unusual appearance does not mean the parent reports the child as otherwise unwell: "[…] She’s definitely not dehydrated and she’s very happy and hitting milestones. […]" [sr003-0052]

