### They ask whether limited smiling, eye contact, gestures or responses mean something is wrong.

12 of 198 posts.

These posts ask about late social smiling, limited eye contact or cooing, not pointing, inconsistent name responses, apparent hearing problems, or little interest in other children. They range from two-month-old babies to an almost 24-month-old at the park; one is written by a caregiving relative about a 14-month-old nephew. Some describe good movement or social skills alongside the concern, and one describes a quiet five-and-a-half-month-old who has already had hearing, iron and genetic tests.

- "Hi! Did anyone else have delayed social smiles from their baby, and how did things turn out? It’s making me anxious. Thanks!" [sr016-0044]
- "[…] The one thing that worries me is this: when I’m holding her face-to-face at my eye level, she hardly ever makes eye contact with me. If she does, it’s only for about 2–5 seconds before looking away." [sr073-0073]
- "My daughter is a little over 12 months old and doesn't point.... if I point to something she looks. She claps, waves etc but won't point" [sr081-0013]
- "What I’m worried about is she doesn’t respond to her name and lot of times she’ll completely ignore us when we talk to her (even if we sing or make funny noises). She smiles at people she knows (when she’s interested in us), won’t smile at strangers despite not having any form of separation anxiety. Also she’s not the best at eye contact." [sr119-0030]
- "Took my almost 24 month to the park and he had no interest in other kids :( is this normal?" [sr107-0073]
- "Iron levels were normal.  
Chromosomal microarray and Fragile X testing came back negative.  
**I'm wondering whether anyone else has had a baby like this who was simply a quiet, laid-back baby or a late bloomer? Did they eventually catch up with their milestones?**  
BEEN TO A PEDIATRICIAN GOING TO ANOTHER ONE ON FRIDAY X" [sr130-0051]

**But:** A limited response in one setting can coexist with strong engagement elsewhere: "The strange thing is that in every other situation, her eye contact seems really good. If she’s lying on her play mat, during tummy time, getting her diaper changed, in front of a mirror, in her car seat, or if someone else is holding her, she’ll happily look at me, smile a lot (especially if I smile first), and seems really engaged." [sr073-0074]

