### They ask whether unusually strong reactions to sounds, sensations or stimulation will pass.

7 of 198 posts.

These posts ask about crying at group laughter, other babies, an adult's tone, cooking, wind or trees, or becoming overwhelmed during play. Ages range from two-to-four-month-old babies to a 24-month-old, including an 11-month-old who became more fearful after hospitalization. Parents describe changing the stimulus, comforting or distracting the child, maintaining social outings, and wondering whether the reaction will fade.

- "[…] But I noticed that if a group of people laugh loudly together, she will cry. Like out of 5 group laughs, she would cry at one of them. Like as if it caught her off guard and she got spooked even if the laughs are back-to-back. and then not cry again even if the laughs continues. Could it be a certain pitch? I noticed it's only when it's a group thing. Like the Happy Birthday song. She will cry when it's sung as a group. We thought it was noise sensitivity, so our family sang it in a low whisper, and she still cried as if it was yelled at her but i noticed its only when it's a sung as a group. […]" [sr051-0057]
- "Hi all, my 11 month old is very sensitive to noise, particularly other babies, regardless of whether they’re happy or sad squeals / noises. He’ll burst into tears and need comforting. He also does this whenever a toy is taken away from him. Once comforted or distracted he’ll then recover very quickly but it breaks my heart, I just want him to enjoy time with other babies." [sr059-0079]
- "i calmed her down and held her and kept trying to put her back down but she kept waking up and this process lasted about 3 hours, i was so tired i started crying and then she lost it again at me crying." [sr113-0051]
- "First we didn't link it to that toy, but now after having seen the same thing happening 6 times we decided to remove the toy and he stopped having these episodes. Then we gave it back to him as a test and he had another episode again." [sr113-0124]
- "I have an 8 month old baby. He has pretty extreme stranger danger and we’ve been trying to tackle that. But, he also is just severely afraid of so many things. We went to the park and he was scared of the wind, literally hyperventilating. He is scared of trees. I feel like we can’t do anything." [sr127-0130]
- "For example, he’d be doing something and my mother would call me from the next room and that scares him and he lets out a cry and crawls to me . Or the doorbell would ring and he gets so scared he lets out a big cry . Or today the phone rang and he did the same thing. I am worried . Is this normal? […]" [sr027-0210]

**But:** Sensitivity can be selective rather than a response to all loud sound: "My daughter is not noise sensitive like blenders or music as we are a Mexican household and always have loudspeakers for parties. […]" [sr051-0057]

