### They ask whether intense tantrums, hitting or risky behaviour are normal for this age.

6 of 198 posts.

Parents ask about hitting, face grabbing, hair pulling, poking, prolonged tantrums or a sudden surge in unsafe confidence. These posts describe children from 13 months to almost two, with one parent's recollection of a first intense tantrum at ten months. Attempts include gentle-hands teaching, leaving after an incident, setting limits and seeking a referral; one parent links worsening meltdowns to being restrained for stitches.

- "I’ve been reading her every book about gentle hands and consent, singing songs and frequently reminders that we don’t touch. When it happens I say we don’t hit/pull hair/grab, check on and apologize to the other child and immediately leave the area. I tell my daughter we are leaving because she’s not being safe and say how she hurt the other child and now they are sad. She will repeat back no hit, little girl hurt, little girl crying but it is still happening." [sr034-0020]
- "He can be super sweet and cuddly, but also throw his stuffies around, squeezes his stuffies, hits me, pokes the dog, try to poke adults mouths and eyeballs… we do our best to correct him of course. […]" [sr039-0027]
- "The tantrums last hours. Longest is 3 hours one night. Pure rage. Back arching. Muscle tensing." [sr061-0115]
- "[…] However, this week seems different. It’s as if someone has turned up his level of confidence way too much. He doesn’t follow rules, runs down the slide without sitting down, and even runs down the stairs. It’s quite scary because when we say no, he throws a huge tantrum, but saying yes is too dangerous now." [sr075-0011]
- "He will tantrum if he cannot do things himself like clicking together his car seat chest strap, closing any door, putting on his own shoes (but he does not even know how???)." [sr083-0065]
- "I don't know what to do, I know toddlers melt down and have tantrums, that's normal, but today and yesterday was BAD. and now that I'm putting 2 and 2 together, I feel so guilty for being frustrated with it all." [sr030-0186]

**But:** Intensity is not always accompanied by a reported developmental problem: "This seems WAY too young for this stuff! Am I going crazy? He is so far presenting at neurotypical, physically healthy, and developmentally is within milestone guidelines." [sr083-0066]

