### They ask whether self-directed habits need stopping because they could hurt their child.

9 of 198 posts.

Parents ask whether grabbing, scratching, hair pulling, head banging or slapping, biting, finger gagging or extensive thumb sucking should be interrupted. Ages range from almost 11 weeks to about a year. Some report bleeding, pulled-out hair or pain, while others describe gentler exploration or worry about eventual tooth effects; they ask how to protect the baby without blocking discovery or self-soothing.

- "My 8 month old son just discovered his “junk”. Over the last 2 weeks he will grab at it incessantly - and not gently!! He will yank/pull and has even left his poor balls bleeding. I try and stop him but he is quick! Is this just a normal part of discovery? I don’t remember my other son ever being this rough!" [sr012-0034]
- "My 6 month old baby doesn’t show very obvious self-soothing habits, but she also will claw at her at her ear if she’s frustrated or tired and sometimes makes herself bleed (I cut her nails whenever I can, as you all know it is not the easiest). She’s also been pulling at her hair pretty intensely while nursing. The clawing at her ear has been happening since like 3 months old so it’s not teething related." [sr016-0024]
- "Basically what do I do… i want to stop her from hurting herself and making herself uncomfortable, but also want her to be able to explore her hands and figure out that she’s in control of them. Do I stop her when she’s aggressively grabbing herself, or just let her do her thing and comfort her if she does hurt herself? […]" [sr066-0037]
- "However… this week he has been sticking his fingers down his throat and gagging. I am constantly having to pull his hand out of his mouth because he does it all the time. It stresses me outtttt." [sr074-0031]
- "My 11 month old is slapping her head by her own hand like 20-30 times everyday. I’m not sure what to do we went to her paediatrician also but still no solution. Anyone else baby did it? When did it end??" [sr114-0030]
- "Everywhere says that thumb sucking is a normal self soothing thing at this age but I don’t know any other babies that do it this much - has anyone else experienced this? I’m starting to get concerned about how it might impact his teeth and wondering if I should intervene to try and stop/ reduce it and how I would even go about it?" [sr093-0063]

**But:** One parent describes the biting as gentle exploration and reports development on track: "I work with children who have different needs and they self harm but it’s much more violent. She seems more like she’s exploring? I’m not sure but it does make me worry a little. She’s ahead of all her physical milestones and seems right on track with cognitive/ language." [sr068-0072]

