### They want reassurance about repetitive movements or fixations.

5 of 198 posts.

These posts ask about toe walking, hand flapping, fascination with hands or repeatedly spinning wheels. Children range from five months to 22 months. Google, a relative's remark or a screening score can turn the behaviour into a worry about autism, while parents also list social abilities and other play; the brief 20-month-old flapping post asks for reassurance without naming a diagnosis.

- "Hi everyone! I’m freaking out. I have a 17 month old who’s been walking normal since she was 12 months. Then out of nowhere she started tip toe walking!! Anytime I go on google I see autism. She talks, she has good eye contact, responds to her name, points, waves, throws kisses everything! But seeing her tip toe walking is freaking me out. Did anybody else go through this?" [sr008-0081]
- "I’m mostly concerned about the first one. Is it a precursor to “finger posturing”? Or just normal baby exploration/fascination? Her next pediatrician appointment isn’t for another month so I’m just wondering if this is normal and especially if anyone else’s LO did/does this?" [sr023-0104]
- "My 22 month old son when he gets extremely angry, he will flap his hands and stomp his feet. My sister immediately went “he probably has autism.”" [sr027-0131]
- "He’s been spinning things since he learned how to spin things lol but it got more obsessive in the last month or so. We can’t even take him on a stroller ride since he wants out to spin the wheels. We just walk with him now. I just don’t know what’s normal and what’s not. […]" [sr030-0112]
- "20 month old son has started flapping a lot and I’m very concerned. Does anyone have some reassurance for me 😢" [sr037-0068]

**But:** The posts include both a zero-risk screening result and a follow-up saying the hand fascination faded: "Thank you. Took it. 0 risk." [sr027-0135] "[…] My baby has mostly grown out of this, she definitely doesn’t get excited by my hands anymore (unless I’m going in for tickles with them haha). […]" [sr023-0105]

