### They ask whether their child's way of playing or exploring is normal.

5 of 198 posts.

Parents ask about fleeting toy attention, roaming and throwing, needing constant entertainment, not raiding cabinets, or repeatedly undoing housework. The children range from ten to 19 months. They describe toy provision, homemade activities, rotations, sensory bins, outings and babyproofing, and ask whether the behaviour is ordinary exploration or a sign of a future problem.

- "[…] My guy has every toy under the sun and plays with essentially none of it. I’ve created little things for him that I’ve seen online and he also plays with none of it. […]" [sr032-0114]
- "[…] However she has never once tried to open the cabinets! She might have touched the handles a bit but when they didn’t open right away she just moved on and doesn’t seem interested at all. There are 4 specific drawers around the house she does like to pull open and raid starting when she was much younger, but it’s almost like if it’s not familiar, she’s not interested. […]" [sr046-0105]
- "[…] She insists on taking all her toys back out of the toy box, when I sweep she runs over to kick the dirt pile, she pulls all her books back off the shelf, gets food simply everywhereee when she eats, throws her stuffies and blankets all over her room, tries to root through the trash can and she also allows absolutely nothing to be left on the coffee table." [sr070-0038]
- "[…] I’ve tried different toy rotations, organizations, toys, activity setups, sensory bins, etc. and TBH I noticed this trait in him very very young even at 6mo old, he would never just sit and play with a toy but instead needed constant stimulation and new toys. […]" [sr098-0118]
- "I have no problem esteem discipline, high standards of manners/public etiquette, or enforcing consequences, but I’m beginning to worry that this energy level and inability to be still and be entertained by toys is going to translate later to behavioral issues at school (especially private school). If you had a similar baby, did you deal with an issue like this as they got older? How did you manage things?" [sr117-0100]

**But:** A child who rejects toys can still engage strongly in particular activities: "[…] The only activity he does now is either go outside and kick a ball/run around or stand on his toddler tower and play with water in the kitchen sink." [sr098-0118]

