### They watch several parts of development at once and worry about a wider developmental problem.

4 of 198 posts.

These posts bring together several concerns rather than one isolated behaviour: few words, little pointing, inconsistent name response, bolting, changing eye contact or repetitive actions. Children range from eight months to the 12-to-18-month toddler stage. Parents weigh reassuring social behaviour against a fear of autism or a wider problem, describe anxiety or disagreement with a partner, and ask about experiences or evaluation.

- "Please be kind because my mental health is failing over this. I’m losing sleep and I can’t enjoy my child because all I do all day is worry and analyze his every move. Every interaction feels like I’m doing an evaluation and I need to stop. To preface, I am in therapy but probably need medication for my anxiety, and he is going to see a speech therapist in a few weeks." [sr003-0184]
- "I'm super anxious that she may have autism. Why she doesn't point? Doesn't talk? Doesn't walk? Why it now seems to me like her eye contact is not as good as before? Why I feel like she sometimes ignore me calling her name? Why she doesn't wave even though I'm trying to teach this gesture for the last few months??? Please share your experiences and thoughts if it sounds like autism or not as alarming." [sr019-0004]
- "I know that I have anxiety issues, My husband was diagnosed with leukemia and we have been in constant fear for the last year. I don't want my daughter to have a problem, I may lose my husband and IDK how I can take care of an autistic kids on my own. Something in her wanting to play on her own, ignoring me, crying a lot and not smiling back in the last few days bothers me. She barely babbles in the last 2 days as well. It feels like she is regressing." [sr019-0007]
- "[…] My bf has made comments before that he thinks something is wrong with her mentally or not all there. I've repeatedly told him that at this age its not something you can actually tell. He said it again when the tantrum behavior changed from flailing limbs to head banging which I try to not to encourage obviously. I told him that the head banging is also unfortunately normal." [sr076-0104]
- "Her dad is autistic level 1, so I know her risk is higher and I’m probably watching everything very closely." [sr117-0009]

**But:** The same posts contain both reassuring professional checks and social responses that do occur: "I did when she was 6 months old, and 1 year old. Both times they checked her joint attention, and at 1 year the ability to put an item in a box after showing how to do it, and told me she is fine. […]" [sr019-0005] "At the same time, she does have social behaviors. She smiles when I come into the room, asks to be picked up, looks for me when scared, […]" [sr117-0007]

