Parents ask about urgently removing crumbs or lint, hiding valued drinks, genital exploration with a toothbrush, chewing caregivers' shoulders despite a recent feed, or hiccups whenever the baby giggles. Children range from about seven-and-a-half months to 18 months. The posts vary from amused curiosity to practical disruption or uncertainty about hunger; the genital-exploration parent says they intend to ask the pediatrician and explicitly says the child is not abused.

- "She will point and yell “UUUUUUUHHHHH!” in increasing intensity and urgency until we we move it… or she will walk around picking stuff up and shove it at us. I have a pocket collection of long-forgotten Cheerios and cat hair by the end of the day." [sr017-0140]
- "She buries her most precious valuables. Like a dog, it’s just buried for later like someone is going to take it away— mainly when it comes to milk or strawberry milk. Does anyone’s tot do this as well? Is it a normal thing or just a quirk? I’ve never seen this personally before and it’s hilarious until I have to dig around her pen to find the very thing she is crying for. 😭" [sr022-0037]
- "My 18mo daughter is giggling while I'm watching TV. I look back and her diaper is off. She has a toothbrush to her private parts (they play with toothbrushes,  mrs Rachels toothbrush song). Again, she is not abused. How do I know? I am with her/them 24/7." [sr082-0078]
- "[…] One thing that does take me back, is her excessive need to gnaw on my chest/shoulder. Whenever anyone holds her, she will open her mouth as wide as she can and go straight for their chest/shoukder, almost as if she is rooting for milk. She’ll do this even if she’s just nursed. And sometimes I’ll try to nurse her again to see if that’s it but she just gets fussy and doesn’t eat. […]" [sr095-0042]
