## Pattern 1, share 06: near posts

### They ask whether suddenly disrupted sleep is a developmental regression that will pass.

11 of 62 posts.

These posts describe sleep changing suddenly after a period that worked: repeated wakes, difficult transfers, false starts, short naps or much longer bedtimes. Children range from ten weeks to almost 17 months, alongside a baby in the six-to-nine-month stage. Parents ask whether this is an early, late or repeated regression, teething, a growth spurt or something they have reinforced, and describe rocking, feeding, contact sleep or changing schedules.

- "My 16 month old is turning 17 months soon and has started having split nights since she turned 15 months. We have done everything we can to adjust her wake windows and nap duration but she will still be up for 2-2.5 hours every night without fail. We dropped a nap, went back to 2 naps, stretched wake windows or shortened them just to figure out if she’s staying up in the middle of the night because of undertiredness or overtiredness but nothing is working." [sr002-0189]
- "My husband and I are trying to figure out if this is the 4 month sleep regression, if we’ve somehow screwed up as parents, or if it’s something else entirely. Please weigh in, fellow parents! 💗" [sr018-0006]
- "My 3 month old was just starting to get in a good routine of only waking up twice at night, once at 1am and once at 4 or 5am. For the past few weeks I cannot transfer her into her bassinet without her waking up 10 mins later. Nothing is working, it usually takes 5 or so tries before I can get her to stay asleep. I'm getting so drained." [sr059-0016]
- "Bedtime has gone from taking 10–15 minutes to an hour and a half. I know it’s only been four days, but it’s really starting to wear me down. […]" [sr072-0098]
- "[…] It’s sending mixed signals 😅 we did nothing different during the day yesterday, our house is roughly the same temperature and he typically sleeps in his own room. Just curious if this will remedy on its own or if he’s clingy now and will continue to struggle for sleep. […]" [sr075-0171]
- "I think the baby has hit the 3-4 month regression at 3 months. I hope so because if not that would mean things could get worse. Baby went from napping 1-4 hours to waking up every 30-45 min, getting fussy when I just put her down to do a task, getting upset when I leave, wanting to be held and much difficult to transfer on the bed/crib." [sr132-0114]

**But:** Disruption is not always all night or continuing at the same intensity: "[…] It doesn’t take too long for her to fall back asleep, and she stays asleep until the morning." [sr018-0005] "[…] last night was better, he slept about 5 hours then 4 hours but was very hard to put down initially to sleep." [sr075-0169]

